1. d4 Nf6 2. g4 {The Kilkenny Gambit! Otherwise known as the 'Gibbins-Weidenhagen Gambit'. This crazy option leads to fascinating gambit play, but a word of warning, this is not for the faint-hearted!} Nxg4 {This is obviously the key move! If Black plays anything else, then they are basically a wimp. The only way to refute a gambit is by accepting it.} (2... d5 {Boring! Thankfully that is not so, we can still make the position quite interesting but aiming to push with e4 at some point.} 3. g5 {Otherwise why did you play g4!? This little pawn is used to kick Black's Knight around.} (3. h3 {Is rather passive.}) 3... Ne4 {It makes sense for Black to occupy the centre of the board.} (3... Nfd7 {Has been played by Eric Hansen, but the Knight is rather passive here and it also gets in the way of Black's pieces.} 4. Nc3 {Sensible developing a piece and creating a threat.} (4. e4 $5 {Is an interest gambit idea and featured in our 'Psycho Kilkenny' course! For this course we pick a more sain option...}) (4. c4 {Is playable if you want to continue in gambit style, but it is not the best move.}) 4... Nb6 {This Knight was blocking Black's pieces, so it needs to be moved.} 5. a4 {As brave Kilkenny players we are always looking at ways to attack, even on the sides of the board! The threat of 6 a4-a5 is a streong threat.} a5 {Black must stop White from playing a4-a5.} 6. Bf4 {Leaves White in a good Jobava London System position. White can continue with h4, h5, e3, Bd3, Nf3, Ne5 etc.} (6. e4 $5 {Is interesting, but not very good for White.})) 4. f3 {The idea in the Kilkenny is to play aggressively and this move kicks the annoying Black Knight on e4 away.} Nd6 5. Nc3 {Developing, creating a threat against Black's pawn on d5 and prepairing to advance in the centre with e4.} e6 {A very popular and sensible move. Black defends his centre and prepares ...Be7.} (5... Nf5 {Has been played by some strong players, and understandable. The Knight was blocking up Black's position on d6 so it moves out of the way.} 6. Bh3 {[%cal Rh3f5,Re2e4] This looks best. White really wants to play e4, but that moves doesn't work here due to ...dxe4 and ...Nxd4. So now the plan is first capture Black's Knight on f5 and then play e4.} c5 {Is an aggressive counter strike, but White is better after} 7. dxc5 {Forcing the pace.} d4 {The only sensible move.} 8. e4 {It is punch Black in the centre of the board.} dxc3 9. Qxd8+ {Ok we do not normally want to exchange Queens, but White is doing rather well after} Kxd8 10. exf5 $14 {A pawn structure that any chess traditionalist would get headaches about.}) (5... c6 {Is the most common move, but White should be happy after} 6. e4 {Gaining charge of the centre and with a simple plan to follow. h4,Be3/f4, Qd2, 0-0-0 and attack.} e6 7. h4 {Charge!} Nd7 8. Bf4 {[%cal Rf4e5] Developing and stopping Blakc form breaking with ...e5.} Be7 9. Qd2 {Not just prepairing 0-0-0 but also the crafty Qh2!? in some situations.} O-O 10. h5 {[%cal Rd2h2,Rg5g6] With a big attack. White has ideas of following up with Qh2 and g6. For example,} b6 11. Qh2 {Attacking a piece and linning up on the h-file.} Ne8 12. g6 {Checkmate incoming!}) (5... Nc6 $5 {This has never been played but it is the engines top choice, the position gets quite spicy after} 6. e4 $5 {Risky, but who wants to live forever!? This blasts the centre open and offers another pawn. We will force Black to accept our gambit!} (6. Bf4 {Is fine, but not as fun as our suggested main move.}) 6... dxe4 7. d5 {Of course! We attack whenever possible, otherwise go and play a more boring opening.} Ne5 8. Qd4 {A nice move, centralising the Queen and forcing the pace.} Nxf3+ {At last we have forced Black to win one of our pawns!} 9. Nxf3 exf3 10. Bd2 {[%cal Re1c1,Ra1d1,Rd1e1,Re1e8] The top engine choice. White has decent compensation for the two pawns with 0-0-0 and Re1 to follow.} (10. Bf4 {Is tempting but the Bishop is actually a bit too exposed on f5.})) 6. e4 {[%cal Gc1e3,Gd1d2,Ge1c1] Gaining space in the centre. Now given a chance White will most likely play Be3, Qd2 and 0-0-0 with a nice position.} Be7 7. h4 {[%cal Rh4g5] Harry comes up the board to look after Garry.} O-O {Where else does Black put the King!?} (7... h6 8. Nh3 {[%cal Rc1e3,Rd1d2,Re1c1] With an interesting struggle ahead. White has more space to maneuver his pieces, and after caslting Queenside with have good chnace to attack Black on the Kingside.}) 8. Bd3 $1 {[%csl Rg8][%cal Rc1e3,Rd1d2,Re1c1,Rg1e2,Ra1g1,Rh4h5,Rg5g6] Now that Black has commited his King, it is time to point the White pieces at it. This is a nice position for White with Be3/f4, Qd2, 0-0-0, Ne2, Rg1, h5, g6 and checkmate to follow.}) 3. e4 {[%csl Gd4,Ge4,Rg4][%cal Rd1g4] This move is the whole point of White's outrageous opening! White takes over the centre at the cost of a pawn, at the same time Black's Knight is now attacked.} d5 {Black's most common response, played about 50% of the time in this position.} (3... d6 {Makes sense. Black takes on a slightly cramped Pirc like position, but with the extra pawn.} 4. Be2 {Developing and attacking attacking the Knight on g4.} (4. Nc3 {Is maybe just as good, but with our move we create a threat.}) 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 {Simple development. As often in the Kilkenny, White needs to play the opening with confidence. Meaning that any fears about being a pawn down need to be totally irrelevant.  White will develop and use his extra space to make Blacks life tricky. Moves such as Nf3, Rg1, Bg5, 0-0-0 will put White in a good position.} e5 {Giving Black some space to breath in the centre.} (5... g6 {Is another set up that White can try. When I would recommend} 6. h4 {Harry!} h5 {Black should stop Harry in its track.} 7. Bg5 Bg7 8. Qd2 {White has nearly got all the troops in the game.} Nc6 9. O-O-O {This happened in K.Delaney vs Z.Kurbanbaev, FIDE World Rapid Team, 2025. White now has ideas such as f4, e5 or even f4 and f5 with the attack.}) 6. Nf3 {Simple chess!} exd4 {The pressure on e5 got too much for Black.} 7. Nxd4 Be7 8. Rg1 {Using the open file.} g6 9. Bh6 $14 {Stopping Black from castling with a great game. This happened in S.Williams vs M.Pankiv, TT, 2026. White will castle Queenside next and then open up the cnetre to attack Black's stranded King.}) (3... Nf6 {Is a werid version of the Alekhine opening. Black is a tempo down, but with an extra pawn.} 4. e5 {The most aggressive move, which we always like!} (4. Nc3 {Is fine, but surely it is more fun pushing Black's Knight around?}) 4... Nd5 (4... Ng8 {Is very passive!} 5. Nf3 {[%cal Rh1g1] Developing and prepairing Rg1.} d6 6. Nc3 {Knights should normally be developed before Bishops.} dxe5 7. Nxe5 $16 {[%cal Rd1f3,Rc1g5,Re1c1,Ra1d1,Rh1g1,Rf1c4] White is doing well. Ideas include Bc4,Qf3, Bg5, 0-0-0, Rg1 etc}) 5. c4 {Push it baby!} Nb6 6. c5 {Keeping the pressure up.} (6. Nc3 {Maybe worth investigating as well, Using the extra space to simple finish developing.}) (6. d5 {Is something that the GingerGM has tried out, but it might be a bit too much}) 6... Nd5 7. Nc3 (7. Bc4 {Was played by Carlsen, with similar ideas to our main suggestion.}) 7... Nxc3 8. bxc3 {With a very strange pawn structure!} d6 {Black needs to break the position up.} 9. cxd6 {[%csl Rc3,Rd4,Re5] Opening up things, but also keeping the pleasant pawn chain c3.d4.e5.} exd6 10. Nf3 {With an interesting battle ahead. White plans on playing Rg1 and even Ng5 with Bc4 to follow attacking f7.}) (3... Nxf2 $2 {This reminds me of two Stags in mating session.  White throws his horns into the air says, "Look at me, I have the biggest horns!". To which Black responds in kind, "No look at me, I am bigger than you!". It turns out that White gets lucky and finds a mate, whilst Black regrets his postering.} 4. Kxf2 {When Black is really just a piece down!}) 4. Be2 {[%csl Rg4][%cal Re2g4] Developing a piece and asking the Knight on g4 what it is going to do.} (4. h3 {Is similar to our main move, but our main move develops a piece as well.}) (4. Bf4 $6 {Is suggested in the 'Psycho-Kilkenny' course!}) (4. Bh3 {Was played by Dmitri Andreikin (Elo 2729) but it is not our recommendation.}) (4. e5 {Is quite often played but not the best move. It was a better idea to threaten something...}) 4... Nf6 {Another move that you can expect, as Black plays this in the majority of cases. The Knight runs away to safety.} (4... h5 {Is a decent move. Black supports the Knight on g4, I would now suggest it is time to try and draw Black's Queen out into the centre.} 5. exd5 {Opening the board up, which suits any gambit player.} (5. h3 {Is interesting but not our main suggestion.}) 5... Qxd5 {[%cal Rd5h1] The Queen recaptures the pawn and attacks White's Rook on h1.} (5... c6 $1 $146 {Has yet to be played, but it does seem like a good move for Black.} 6. dxc6 {Restoring material equality.} Nxc6 7. Nf3 {The pawn on d4 needs to be defended.} Bf5 8. h3 {It is time to kick that annoying piece away.} Nf6 9. Be3 {With a strange structure. Black may have a slightly preferrable position but it is all to play for. White can aim for Bd2, Nbd2, Nc4 and Ne5.}) 6. Nf3 {White will now aim to gain tempo against Black's Queen, whilst at the same time developing pieces with moves such as Nc3. This plan seems to give White good gambit style compensation. For example,} Nc6 $6 {This natural developing move is actually a mistake.} (6... c6 {Has been played by a Grandmaster, the idea is to stop White from advancing with d4-d5. This does allow White to gain a number of tempo though, for example Williams vs Nevednichy, ECC Open, 2016, continued} 7. Nc3 {Hello Queenie, "what you gonna do, when they come for you".} Qa5 {Pinning the Knight on c3.} 8. Bc4 {[%cal Rc4f7] The Bishop moves to an aggressive square, looking over at the f7 point.} Bf5 {Black also needs to develop.} 9. Bd2 {[%csl Rc3][%cal Rc3d5] Setting up a discovered attack with the Knight on c3.} e6 10. Qe2 {White should be happy. With Queenside castling to follow and numerous ways to open Black's position up.} Qd8 11. O-O-O $16 {White is now ready to break the Black King open.} Be7 12. h3 {It is time to ask Black's Knight to kindly remove itself from our area of the board.} Nf6 13. Nh4 $1 {The key move. Black's Bishop doesn't have a comfortable square to move to.} Bh7 14. Rhg1 {Using the open g-file.} g6 {[%csl Rh7] What else? Now we should take a moment to laugh at the Bishop on h7.} 15. Nf3 $16 {The Knight comes back and aims for either e5 or g5. With a great position.}) 7. Nc3 {A tempo is a tempo} Qd7 {Where ever the Queen moves to, White's next move is the same.} 8. d5 {Attack! This gains more time by pushing Black's Knight away.} Nce5 (8... Nb8 $2 {Happened in Williams vs Sayce, TT, 2026. But this is a very passive retreat.} 9. Qd4 $1 {Is now winning for White! Black is struggling to find a good move and White can increase the pressure with ideas such as Bf4, 0-0-0, h3, Ne5 etc}) 9. Nxe5 {This simple capture works well here} Nxe5 10. Qd4 $1 {Pushing the Knight further away. White is doing great, with ideas such as Rg1, Bf4, 0-0-0 and even Bb5 to follow.}) (4... Nh6 {Was played in A.Tang vs A.Firouzja, TT, 2021.} 5. Bxh6 {Now looks like a simple plan. Swapping an undeveloped piece for a developed piece, whilst at the same time damaging Black's pawn structure.} gxh6 6. exd5 {This is a common idea, when Black can only recapture with the Queen. The Black Queen will be exposed to attack on d5.} Qxd5 7. Nf3 {The Knight develops and blocks Black's Queen from playing ...Qxh1+.} Nc6 $6 {Is not a great square for the Knight.} (7... Bf5 8. Nc3 {Gaining time on the Queen.} Qd8 9. Qd2 {[%cal Re1c1,Ra1d1,Rd2e3,Rf3e5] White has a great gambit position, with 0-0-0, Qe3 and Ne5 to follow.}) 8. Nc3 {Gaining time by attacking Black's Queen.} Qd8 9. d5 {This is the problem with Black's last move ...Nc6. The move d5 comes with a great initiative. In actual fact, Black is now losing!} Nb8 {Where else!?} 10. Rg1 {With a craft idea in mind... Can you see the idea?} e6 $2 {Black was already in trouble, but this hastens the end.} 11. Qd4 $1 {Black's Rook on h8 has nowhere to go!} f6 12. O-O-O {With Qh4-Qh5+ to follow. Who has the more developed pieces here!? (Am I allowed to laugh...)}) (4... Nxf2 $2 {Better than a move ago, and the kind of move that a drunk machine gun controller would play. As long as White doesn't not panic this idea doesn't work.} 5. Kxf2 {Not much choice there...} dxe4 (5... e5 $6 {The Black Queen wants to reach h4, but it is hard to checkmate with just the Queen...} 6. exd5 Qh4+ 7. Kf1 exd4 8. Qe1 $16) 6. h4 {This rules out any ...Qh4+ ideas. The White King should always feel pretty secure on g2, if it ever needs to move there.}) (4... Qd7 {Is very rare and not really worth worrying about.} 5. exd5 {This is a typical idea, gambit players love opening up the position and this move opens things up!} (5. e5 {Is now sensible as the Black Bishop on c8 is stuck behind the Queen, but we prefer a more violent option...}) 5... Qxd5 6. Nf3 {With Nc3 and quick development to follow.}) 5. e5 {It is time to ask the Black Knight where it wants to move to?!} Nfd7 {The Knight is not ideal here, as it blocks several of Blacks pieces in. White needs to develop and keep the pressure up.} (5... Ng8 {What a journey that Knight has had! Bravely jumping to g4 and then running home again, like a drunk Viking. This retreat has been preferred by several strong players and it is a good move. On g8 the Knight doesn't block any of Black's pieces (which is not the case had it been moved to d7) and it has chances to later sit on the f5 square.} 6. c4 {This push is an Andrew Tang 'The Bullet King' specialality. White needs to keep the pressure against Black's position up.} dxc4 (6... e6 {Is sensible. Black just tries to keep a solid structure.} 7. Nf3 {This is part of our normal plan. we develop and leave the g1 square free for the Rook. My honest feeling is that White has the easier posiiton to play, but Black should be ok with perfect play. For example} Ne7 8. Rg1 {All part of the Kilkenny plan!} Nf5 9. Nc3 {White must develop before attacking. Now lets take a look at a move that could be quite common in order to create a solid pawn structure.} c6 10. Bg5 {Every White piece has now entered the game.} Be7 $2 {This normal move is actually a mistake.} 11. Bxe7 {[%cal Rg1g7] Clearing the g-file for White's Rook.} Qxe7 12. Bd3 $1 {The key move. Now White is threatening Bxf5 and Rxg7.} g6 {The only way to stop White's plan.} 13. Bxf5 {Black's Knight was his only developed piece, so it makes sense to exchange it off.} gxf5 14. Qd2 {White is doing great. The Queen is on it's way to h6 with 0-0-0, Rg7 and Ng5 to follow.}) (6... c5 $2 {Has been played by the strong Grandmasters Jakub Kosakowski and Dmitri Andreikin, but it is a mistake and with correct play White is doing very well!} 7. cxd5 $1 {This just looks good, bringing Black's Queen into the open.} Qxd5 8. Nf3 cxd4 9. Nc3 {With a plan...} Qa5 {We have been following A.Tang vs D.Andreikin and now White should have played} 10. Qxd4 $1 {White has great development.} Nc6 11. Qe4 {White is a lot better! For example,} e6 12. a3 Nge7 13. b4 Qd8 14. Bg5 h6 15. Rd1 Qb6 16. Be3 Qc7 17. Nb5) (6... Nc6 {Was played in J.Xiong vs A.Firouzja, TT, 2022.} 7. Nf3 {This is nearly always a good idea, with Rg1 to follow next.} e6 (7... Bg4 {Seems normal but it does run into} 8. Rg1 {Forcing the Bishop to make a decision.} Bxf3 9. Bxf3 {White has good pressure on the light squares here and should be happy with how things have gone.} dxc4 (9... e6 $6 {Is an error due to} 10. cxd5 exd5 11. Nc3 {When Black has problems defending d5.}) 10. Be3 {White will follow up with ideas such as Qa4, Nc3, 0-0-0 and hopefully d5.})) (6... Bf5 {Is a sensible square for the Bishop.} 7. cxd5 {This is such a common plan. A basic rule could be "When Black can only recapture on d5 with the Queen, White should generally play cxd5".} Qxd5 8. Bf3 {Defending and attacking.} Qa5+ (8... Be4 $5 {Allows} 9. Nc3 Bxf3 10. Nxf3 {White has great development and a strong centre, which is enough compensation for a little pawn.}) 9. Nc3 c6 10. Nge2 {With good compensation. White will follow up with Bd2, Ng3 and even h4-h5.}) (6... c6 {Tries to hold the centre together.} 7. Nc3 {Putting pressure back on the centre.} e6 (7... dxc4 {Is always an option for Black, but it does free up White's pieces. For example,} 8. Bxc4 e6 9. Qg4 $5 {White is very active and can just keep developing pieces to good natural squares. For example,} Ne7 10. Bg5 {Developing and pinning Black's Knight down to e7.} h6 11. Ne4 $1 {With such a lead in development it is time to look for the final kill! Thaty d6 square is very juicy, now one fun line continues} hxg5 12. Nd6+ Kd7 13. Nxf7 Qa5+ 14. Kf1 {White is threatening the Rook and ideas with the e6 pawn.} Rh4 {Saving the Rook but not the King!} 15. Qxe6+ Kc7 16. Qd6+ {The end is near...} Kb6 17. Qd8#) 8. Nf3) 7. Bxc4 {White has open play for his pieces and easy developing moves. But Black is solid and hard to break down. Play could continue} e6 8. Nf3 {This followed up with Rg1 is often the right plan.} Ne7 9. Rg1 {With compensation. White will just keep developing with moves such as Nc3, Bg5, Qd2/e2, 0-0-0.}) (5... Ne4 $2 {Is just a blunder! Sometimes you must take a step back in life, in order to move forwards.} 6. f3 {White wins a piece and Black has little compensation.}) 6. Nf3 {There are some tempting options here, but this is a good and sensible move. White needs to develop pieces. You can't attack the enemy with a handful of fresh air! (maybe you can if you're a Ninja)} (6. e6 {Is cowboy style! M.Carlsen vs G.Caprio, 2025, TT.}) (6. c4 {Is also interesting, but not as good as the simple move we are suggesting.}) 6... e6 {[%csl Rf8] Black reinforces the centre and prepares to develop the Bishop on f8.} (6... c5 {Is aiming for immediate action, but White is better developed to deal with such outbreaks.} 7. c4 $1 {Remember that this move and the general opening of the position should be something that we always want to occur.} dxc4 8. d5 {Of yes, just look at those beautiful pawns!} Qc7 9. Bf4 {White's moves are so natural. Defend and develop!} b5 10. Nc3 {Another one comes into the game.} a6 11. d6 {With a big attack.}) 7. Bg5 {The most aggressive move! White attacks Black's Queen and forces ...Be7. After ...Be7 Black will have a weakness on g7.} (7. c4 {Is ok, but I would prefer to attack Black's Queen.}) (7. Rg1 {Can never be bad and is one of the reasons that we sacrificed our g-pawn! Saying that there is a stronger move in the position. Think about developing and attacking our opponents Queen.}) 7... Be7 (7... f6 {Opens Black's King up so is a very risky move.} 8. exf6 {As we are attacking it makes sense to open the position as much as possible.} Nxf6 9. Nc3 {[%cal Rd1d2,Re1c1,Rh1g1] Finishing development. Our future plan is now Qd2, 0-0-0, Rg1 when we have every piece ready to attack.} Bd6 10. Qd2 {[%cal Re1c1] Prepairing to castle Queenside.} O-O {This is a too risky, but demonstrates White's attack well.} 11. O-O-O {Now everything is ready.} Nc6 12. Rhg1 {[%cal Rg1g8] One of the main reasons we play 2 g4 is for this file!} Bd7 13. Rg2 {[%cal Rd1g1,Rg1g8] Prepairing Rdg1 with huge presence on the g-file. White is doing great, I doubt Black's King can survive much longer.}) 8. Rg1 {[%csl Rg7][%cal Rg1g7] Eyeing up the g7 pawn.} Bxg5 (8... O-O {Is asking for trouble.} 9. Bh6 {Is the simplest, winning material. As Black has no good way to defend g7 so much play} g6 {Allowing} 10. Bxf8 {When White has won points.}) 9. Rxg5 {[%csl Rg7][%cal Rg5g7] The correct way to capture, as now g7 is attacked.} (9. Nxg5 {Is an option, but there is no follow up threat.}) 9... g6 {Defending the weak point.} (9... O-O {Is very risky due to the open g-file.} 10. Bd3 {Bringing another piece to the party. White lines up everything to look at Blakc's King.} Nc6 11. Nbd2 {White wants to play Qe2, 0-0-0 and Rdg1 with the attack.} Nxd4 {[%cal Rd8g5]} 12. Bxh7+ {Fighting tactics with tactics!} Kxh7 13. Rh5+ {Now the Rook has moved out of the way of Black's Queen.} Kg8 14. Nxd4 {[%cal Rd1g4,Rg4h3,Rh5h8,Re1c1,Ra1g1,Rg1g8] With a crazy position where Black's King is in more danger. White will follow up with ideas such as Qg4, 0-0-0 and Rg1.}) 10. Qd2 {[%cal Re1c1,Rd2f4] A useful move. The Queen can now move over to the Kingside and White is one step nearer to castling Queenside.} (10. c4 {Is possible, but White can keep this idea as an option for later on.}) 10... c5 {If Black doesn't do anything then White will finish developing and use the extra space to attack.} 11. dxc5 $1 {A good decision, opening the position often helps White in the Kilkenny.} (11. Nc3 {Is possible but not as strong as another option...}) 11... Nxc5 12. Nc3 {[%csl Re4][%cal Rc3e4,Rc5e4,Re1c1] Stopping ...Ne4 and prepairing to castle.} Nc6 13. O-O-O {White has completed development whilst Black's King is still unsure about where to go.} a6 {This move stops Nb5.} 14. Rg4 {[%cal Rd2h6,Rh6g7] This is clever and does several things. - Steps out of the way of Black's Queen. - Makes way for Qh6-g7 and Ng5. - Helps things on the 4th rank.} (14. Qe3) 14... b5 15. Qh6 {In it goes! White is starting to create some threats. Qg7 is putting some pressure on Black.} Qe7 {Black must try to castle Queenside. This prepares that.} (15... Bb7 $2 {This natural move is actually a mistake due to} 16. Qg7 {Forcing Black to play} Rf8 17. Ng5 $1 $16 {Is now very strong. The simple threat is Nxh7 and Qxf8+}) 16. b4 $1 {White must keep creating threats! We want to do this before Black can castle.} Nd7 {There is nowhere better. It is best that the Knight stays near Black's King to help defend it.} 17. Rxd5 $3 {Boom! Ripping things open. Put that in your pipe and smoke it!} exd5 (17... Bb7 18. Rd2 {The safest square for White's Rook.} (18. Rd6 $2 {Is a mistake due to 18...Ndxe5}) 18... Nxb4 (18... Ncxe5 $4 {Is a blunder due to} 19. Nxe5 Nxe5 20. Qg7 $1 $18 {Attacking both the Rook and Knight.}) 19. Rd6 {White has an ongoing attack. The threat is now Rxb4 and Black has yet to get his King safe.} (19. Rgd4 {Is another interesting option.})) 18. Nxd5 {With a big attack!} Qf8 (18... Qd8 $2 19. e6 $1 {Another brilliant move, opening up more lines!} fxe6 20. Rxg6 $1 {Magic after magic!} exd5 21. Re6+ {It is time for the final kill.} Ne7 (21... Kf7 22. Rxc6 {Balck's King is too weak, White is winning.}) 22. Nd4 {[%cal Re2h5] Making way for Bh5+. White is totally winning.}) 19. Qe3 {White has a very strong attack. The main threats are Nc7+ and e6.} *

